# What Vercel is actually for
# --------------------------
# This project deploys to two places, and they do different jobs:
#
#   * GitHub Pages (matchdayterminal.com) serves the site. deploy.yml's
#     "Assemble public site (curated allowlist only)" step copies a hand-picked
#     set of files into _site/ and publishes that. It is the canonical origin --
#     index.html's <link rel="canonical"> points there.
#
#   * Vercel serves ONLY the serverless API under api/ -- /api/leaderboard,
#     /api/auth and /api/auth-callback, backed by Postgres through `pg`.
#     app-1-core.js reaches it at matchday-lake-omega.vercel.app and nowhere
#     else. package.json is even named "matchday-leaderboard".
#
# Without this file Vercel uploaded the whole repository on every push, and
# deploy.yml pushes a ratings/picks commit roughly hourly (374 commits in the
# last 30 days). That is ~47 MB of ledgers, archives, Python and social images
# re-uploaded a dozen times a day to serve four JavaScript files -- which is
# what filled the account's storage.
#
# It also meant the Vercel root served a broken copy of the site: the tracked
# index.html still contains the literal __BUILD__ placeholder (only _site's
# copy gets stamped with the commit SHA), and data.json is gitignored so it
# never arrived. Nothing links to that root. Dropping it loses nothing.
#
# Deny everything, then re-admit only what the functions need. Same curated
# allowlist approach deploy.yml already uses for the public site -- a new
# ledger, cache or archive is excluded by default instead of silently
# inflating the next deployment.
/*

# The serverless functions themselves, plus the shared account/session helper
# they all import (api/_accounts.js).
!/api

# Declares the `pg` dependency the functions import. Vercel installs from this.
!/package.json

# Response headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, cache-control) applied to /api/*.
!/vercel.json
